+<section class="article-hero">
+<div class="article-hero-inner">
+<div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="category-indy.html">Indy</a><span>/</span>Four Restaurants That Became Institutions</div>
+<div class="article-cat-badge">Indy</div>
+<h1 class="article-headline">Four Indianapolis Restaurants <em>That Became Institutions</em></h1>
+<p class="article-deck">Different food, different decades, different parts of town. What St. Elmo, the Slippery Noodle, Harry &amp; Izzy's, and Bluebeard have in common is that Indianapolis would actually notice if any of them closed.</p>
+<p class="article-meta">I'll Ask For It · Indy · One Man's Opinion</p>
+</div>
+</section>
+<section class="article-body">
+<div class="article-body-inner">
+<div class="prose">
+<p>Every city has restaurants. Not every city has restaurants that have become part of how the city describes itself. Indianapolis has four of the second kind, and they didn't get there the same way. One did it with a shrimp cocktail that requires a bib. One did it by being the oldest bar in the state and never redecorating. One did it by being the reliable answer when the first answer is booked. One did it by proving Mass Ave was ready before Mass Ave knew it.</p>
+<p>These aren't the only good restaurants in Indianapolis. They're the four that have crossed over from "good restaurant" to "the restaurant Indianapolis points to when it has to prove it has restaurants."</p>
+</div>
+<h3>St. Elmo Steak House</h3>
+<div class="prose">
+<p>The shrimp cocktail will make your eyes water, and that's the point — the horseradish sauce is famous enough that people order it as a dare. The steaks are enormous. The dining room still feels like 1902, because large parts of it are. This is the restaurant Indianapolis reaches for when it needs to show someone it's a real restaurant town, and it has been doing that job for well over a century.</p>
+</div>
+<h3>Slippery Noodle Inn</h3>
+<div class="prose">
+<p>The oldest continuously operating bar in Indiana, open since 1850 under one name or another. Blues seven nights a week. You can feel the decades in the floor before anyone tells you the history. Some bars have a backstory they lead with. The Slippery Noodle doesn't need to — the building does the talking.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="pull-quote"><p>"Some places got here on the food. One got here on just refusing to close since 1850."</p><cite>I'll Ask For It · Indy</cite></div>
+<h3>Harry &amp; Izzy's</h3>
+<div class="prose">
+<p>St. Elmo's steakhouse cousin, and the rare sequel that does everything right on its own terms. The shrimp cocktail is famous for the same reason the original's is. The pork chops are an argument-ender. It's the answer when St. Elmo's is booked and somehow never feels like the consolation prize.</p>
+</div>
+<h3>Bluebeard</h3>
+<div class="prose">
+<p>The restaurant that proved Mass Ave was ready for something more ambitious than it was getting. Farm-to-table that doesn't lecture anyone about it. Creative, consistent, and still the menu people point to when they explain what changed in Indianapolis dining over the last decade.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="verdict-box">
+<p class="verdict-label">↑ The Verdict</p>
+<h2 class="verdict-title">DIFFERENT ROOMS. SAME STANDARD.</h2>
+<p class="verdict-text">St. Elmo for the occasion. The Slippery Noodle for the history. Harry &amp; Izzy's for the reliable excellent night out. Bluebeard for proof the city keeps getting better. Four different reasons to trust a restaurant, and Indianapolis has all four covered.</p>
+</div>
+</div>
+</section>
